                <pb facs="1112" n="1102" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg1102"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">641.0031/131</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Canada</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Clark</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1469</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-07-01">July 1, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Secretary of State refers to the Legation’s airgram no. A–20 of June 18, 1943
                    reporting Mr. Norman Robertson’s comments on the current London discussions and
                    on post-war commercial policy.</p>
                <p>The Legation is requested, if it perceives no objection, to take a suitable
                    opportunity to ask Mr. Robertson to elaborate on his comments relative to
                    multilateral agreements and bilateral agreements. It would be helpful, for
                    instance, if he would indicate by concrete examples a little more specifically
                    what he means by expressing the feeling that multilateral agreements might be on
                    broad bases implemented by bilateral agreements.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
